STANCES=[
 (110, "Batching policy is the dominant confounder, because continuous batching changes the per-step token count that every collective is amortised over.",
  "With continuous (in-flight) batching the decode step processes whatever requests are resident, so the batch dimension of every GEMM and the payload of every all-reduce move with arrival rate. Tensor parallelism pays a fixed per-collective latency that is amortised over the whole step batch, so its relative cost falls as the resident batch grows; pipeline parallelism instead needs enough independent micro-batches to keep stages fed. Comparing the two arms under different scheduler settings measures the scheduler, not the layout.",
  "H110: at fixed layout the tensor arm's per-token collective overhead as a fraction of step time will fall monotonically with resident batch size, so the tensor-versus-pipeline gap measured at low arrival rate will not predict the gap at production arrival rate (ESTIMATE; derivation: per-step collective latency is roughly batch-independent at decode payload sizes, while useful compute per step grows with resident batch, so the overhead ratio falls as one over batch).",
  "Pin the scheduler configuration - maximum resident sequences, maximum batched tokens, chunked-prefill on or off, preemption policy - identically across both arms, then sweep arrival rate alone across at least four load points spanning below, at and above the SLO knee.",
  ["Scheduler configuration dump for every arm, showing identical batching limits",
   "Resident batch size distribution per step, not a nominal maximum",
   "Collective time as a fraction of step time at each load point",
   "TTFT p95 and TPOT p95 versus arrival rate for both arms, with the SLO knee identified"],
  ["A single-load-point comparison generalises to no other load point",
   "Different scheduler defaults between arms silently make this a scheduler experiment",
   "Chunked prefill shifts cost between TTFT and TPOT and can flip which SLO binds"],
  0.62),
 (111, "Prefill and decode have opposite parallelism preferences, so a single blended verdict hides two contradictory answers.",
  "Prefill is compute-bound and highly parallel across the sequence dimension: it amortises collectives well and scales with tensor degree until compute saturates. Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound with a tiny per-step payload, so it is latency-dominated by the same collectives. A workload with long prompts and short outputs therefore prefers a different layout than one with short prompts and long outputs, and a pooled metric averages the two into a number that describes neither.",
  "H111: separating the measurement into prefill-only and decode-only phases will show the tensor arm's advantage larger in prefill than in decode, and the pooled verdict will track whichever phase dominates the prompt-to-output token ratio of the traffic used (ESTIMATE; derivation: collective cost per useful FLOP is far lower in prefill because the sequence dimension multiplies the compute per collective, while decode carries one collective pair per layer per single token).",
  "Instrument prefill and decode time separately per request, then run three traffic mixes with deliberately different prompt-to-output ratios while holding layout, engine and scheduler fixed. Report the layout gap per phase and per mix, never pooled only.",
  ["Per-request prefill time and decode time recorded separately, not just end-to-end latency",
   "Prompt-length and output-length distributions for each traffic mix",
   "Layout gap reported per phase, with the pooled number shown only alongside the split",
   "The production prompt-to-output ratio, to identify which phase actually dominates"],
  ["A pooled latency number lets a prefill-driven result be cited as a decode conclusion",
   "Synthetic mixes with equal prompt and output lengths match almost no real workload",
   "Optimising the phase that is not the bottleneck yields no user-visible improvement"],
  0.63),
 (112, "Disaggregated prefill and decode dissolves the single-layout question, because each pool can then take the layout its phase prefers.",
  "If prefill and decode run in separate pools, the KV cache produced by prefill must be transferred to the decode pool, and that transfer becomes a new first-class cost on the critical path for TTFT. The benefit is that each pool is independently sized and independently sharded; the cost is a KV transfer whose latency scales with per-request KV bytes over the achievable link bandwidth, plus a new failure mode when the transfer path degrades.",
  "H112: disaggregation will improve decode TPOT p95 at fixed hardware while worsening TTFT p95 by approximately the KV transfer time, computed as per-request KV bytes divided by achieved transfer bandwidth (ESTIMATE; derivation: decoding no longer contends with prefill compute, but the handoff inserts a serial transfer whose duration is the transferred working set over the realised, not nominal, link bandwidth).",
  "Run colocated and disaggregated arms at identical total device count and identical traffic, and instrument the KV transfer as its own timed phase. Sweep transfer path quality by restricting bandwidth in a controlled way to establish the sensitivity.",
  ["Per-request KV bytes transferred and achieved transfer bandwidth, both measured",
   "TTFT decomposed into queue, prefill, KV transfer and first-token phases",
   "Identical total device count across colocated and disaggregated arms, documented",
   "Behaviour of the disaggregated arm when the transfer path is degraded or saturated"],
  ["Nominal link bandwidth overstates achieved transfer rate, often by a wide margin",
   "Disaggregation adds a cross-pool dependency that turns one pool's incident into both pools' incident",
   "A TPOT win reported without the TTFT regression is an incomplete and misleading result"],
  0.60),
 (113, "Prefix caching changes the measured baseline, because a warm cache removes prefill work that the layout comparison was implicitly measuring.",
  "Shared-prefix reuse skips recomputation of cached blocks, so the effective prompt length seen by the engine falls and the workload shifts toward decode. If one arm is benchmarked with a warm cache and the other cold, the comparison is dominated by cache hit rate rather than by layout. Hit rate is itself traffic-dependent and drifts as prompt templates change.",
  "H113: reported cache hit rate will explain more of the variance between two runs of the same arm than the layout difference explains between arms at fixed hit rate (ESTIMATE; derivation: a cache hit removes the entire prefill cost of the matched prefix, which is typically a larger fraction of end-to-end time than the layout-attributable difference in collective overhead).",
  "Record cache hit rate per run and either pin it by warming identically before every arm or report results stratified by hit-rate band. Re-run each arm cold and warm to bound the effect before comparing layouts at all.",
  ["Prefix-cache hit rate per run, reported alongside every latency number",
   "Explicit warm-up procedure applied identically to every arm",
   "Cold and warm results for each arm, so the cache effect size is bounded",
   "Prompt-template distribution, since hit rate depends on prefix sharing in traffic"],
  ["An unreported hit-rate difference can invert the apparent layout ranking",
   "Cache state carries across runs and silently couples supposedly independent trials",
   "Hit rate measured on replayed traffic overstates what live traffic will achieve"],
  0.61),
 (114, "Quantization must be held fixed, because numeric format changes both the memory pressure that motivates sharding and the kernel paths being compared.",
  "Lower-precision weights and KV shrink per-device footprint, which can make a lower tensor degree feasible and therefore remove the reason to shard at all; they also route execution through different kernels whose efficiency varies by shape and by degree. Changing precision and layout together produces a result attributable to neither, and can also change output quality, which no latency metric will reveal.",
  "H114: at fixed layout, moving to a lower-precision weight or KV format will reduce per-device memory bytes roughly in proportion to the bit-width ratio while changing latency non-proportionally, because the kernel path and any dequantization overhead differ by shape (ESTIMATE; derivation: storage scales directly with bit width, whereas achieved throughput depends on kernel support for the specific shard shape, which need not track the same ratio).",
  "Hold the numeric format constant across the layout comparison, then run a separate precision sweep at the winning layout only. Pair every precision arm with a task-quality evaluation, not latency alone.",
  ["Exact weight and KV numeric formats, and the quantization method, for every arm",
   "Per-device memory bytes and achieved latency reported separately per format",
   "A task-quality evaluation for every precision change, on a held-out set",
   "Kernel selection logs showing which path each shard shape actually took"],
  ["Precision changes can silently degrade output quality with no latency signal at all",
   "A quantized model may fit without sharding, invalidating the premise of the comparison",
   "Kernel efficiency is shape-dependent, so precision and shard degree interact non-linearly"],
  0.62),
 (115, "Multi-tenancy on shared devices invalidates isolated benchmarks, because a co-resident workload competes for the same bandwidth and the same collectives.",
  "A layout validated on an idle node behaves differently when another replica, a monitoring agent or a second model shares devices or the fabric. Contention lands on memory bandwidth, on the interconnect and on scheduler queues, and the arm with more synchronous collective traffic is hurt more because its critical path includes waiting on the slowest participating rank.",
  "H115: introducing a controlled co-resident load will degrade the higher-collective-count arm more than the other, and the degradation will scale with the co-tenant's interconnect usage rather than with its raw compute usage (ESTIMATE; derivation: synchronous collectives block on the slowest rank, so contention that delays any single rank propagates to the whole step, whereas independent compute contention degrades more gracefully).",
  "Run each arm alone and against a calibrated co-tenant of known interconnect and compute intensity, holding everything else fixed. Report degradation per arm rather than absolute latency only.",
  ["Documented isolation state: what else ran on the node and fabric during each run",
   "A calibrated co-tenant with independently characterised compute and interconnect intensity",
   "Per-arm degradation from isolated to contended conditions",
   "Fabric utilisation counters during the contended runs"],
  ["Benchmarks on an idle node systematically overstate production performance",
   "Contention effects are asymmetric across layouts, so a single derating factor is wrong",
   "Monitoring and logging agents are co-tenants too and are routinely omitted from the accounting"],
  0.60),
 (116, "Statistical discipline decides whether the comparison says anything, because layout gaps are often smaller than run-to-run variance.",
  "A single run per arm yields a point estimate with unknown spread, and serving latency distributions are heavy-tailed and autocorrelated. Without repeats, a declared warm-up, a fixed measurement window and an interval on the difference, a reported gap cannot be distinguished from noise, and the risk of shipping a change that does nothing is high.",
  "H116: the between-repeat standard deviation of TPOT p95 within a single arm will be of the same order as the reported gap between arms, so a single-run comparison will not support the claim it is used for (ESTIMATE; derivation: tail latency estimators over finite windows carry sampling variance that grows with the percentile and with autocorrelation in arrivals, which is precisely the regime of a p95 measured over a short run).",
  "Run at least five independent repeats per arm with process restarts between them, discard a declared warm-up window, and report the difference with a bootstrap interval rather than as a point value. Pre-register the effect size that would count as meaningful.",
  ["At least five independent repeats per arm, with restarts between repeats",
   "Declared warm-up window and fixed measurement window, applied identically",
   "Bootstrap or equivalent interval on the between-arm difference",
   "A pre-registered minimum meaningful effect size, declared before the runs"],
  ["A gap smaller than within-arm variance is not a finding and must not be shipped as one",
   "Percentile estimators over short windows are unstable and easy to over-read",
   "Choosing the measurement window after seeing results manufactures significance"],
  0.64),
 (117, "Cost per sustained token, not latency alone, is the decision variable, because a latency win bought with more devices may be the worse choice.",
  "Layouts differ in how many devices a single replica occupies and in how much SLO-sustaining throughput each replica delivers. The comparable quantity is devices required to serve the target rate within the SLO, which combines latency, achievable concurrency and replica granularity. A layout that is slightly faster per request but forces coarser replica sizing can raise total device count and stranded capacity.",
  "H117: ranking the arms by devices needed to sustain the target rate within the SLO can differ from ranking them by single-request latency, because achievable concurrency per device and replica granularity differ between layouts (ESTIMATE; derivation: required devices equals target rate divided by per-replica SLO-sustaining rate, rounded up to replica granularity, and neither the divisor nor the rounding is determined by single-request latency).",
  "For each arm, find the maximum arrival rate sustaining the SLO, derive devices required for the target rate including replica-granularity rounding, and compare that number rather than latency. Hold SLO definition and traffic identical.",
  ["SLO-sustaining maximum rate per replica for each arm, measured not assumed",
   "Devices per replica and the resulting replica-granularity rounding for the target rate",
   "Stranded capacity implied by each arm's replica granularity",
   "A single written SLO definition applied identically to both arms"],
  ["Optimising latency in isolation can raise total device count and cost",
   "Peak throughput measured without the SLO constraint is not a usable capacity number",
   "Coarse replica granularity strands capacity that never appears in a latency chart"],
  0.63),
 (118, "Observability must be in place before the experiment, because an unattributable result costs another full experiment cycle.",
  "If per-phase timings, collective time, memory occupancy, queue depth and throttle state are not captured during the run, a surprising result cannot be explained and the run must be repeated. The instrumentation itself perturbs the measurement, so its overhead must be quantified and held identical across arms rather than enabled on one side only.",
  "H118: adding the full instrumentation set will change measured latency by a small but non-zero amount, and that overhead will be similar across arms, so it can be held as a constant rather than corrected per arm (ESTIMATE; derivation: sampling-based counters add a roughly fixed per-interval cost independent of layout, whereas tracing every collective would add cost proportional to collective count and would not be layout-neutral).",
  "Measure each arm with instrumentation off and on before the main comparison to quantify overhead, then run the comparison with instrumentation identically enabled on both arms.",
  ["Instrumentation-off versus instrumentation-on latency for each arm, to quantify overhead",
   "Per-phase timings, collective time, KV occupancy, queue depth and throttle flags captured per run",
   "Confirmation that identical instrumentation was enabled on every arm",
   "Retained raw traces, so a surprising result can be re-examined without re-running"],
  ["Enabling profiling on one arm only biases the comparison toward the uninstrumented arm",
   "Uninstrumented runs produce results that cannot be explained and must be repeated",
   "Full per-collective tracing is not layout-neutral and can distort the very gap under test"],
  0.61),
 (119, "State plainly what this record is and is not, so it cannot be mistaken for adjudicated truth or for evidence of model capability.",
  "This item is provisional teacher-B review output written by a single reviewing model in a blind lane, without sight of any other lane's answer and without executing any benchmark. Its worth is as a structured, falsifiable design argument with explicit evidence requirements attached, not as a verdict on the underlying question. Every quantitative claim in this batch is labelled ESTIMATE and carries its derivation; nothing here is MEASURED, because no run was performed to produce it.",
  "H119: an independent reviewer given the same prompt and the same evidence policy will reproduce the qualitative default - tensor parallelism inside the node, pipeline parallelism only to cross a node boundary or to fit an otherwise infeasible model - while disagreeing on specific numeric thresholds (ESTIMATE; derivation: the qualitative default follows from the collective-count and bubble-fraction mechanisms, which are structural, whereas thresholds depend on hardware and engine specifics the prompt does not fix).",
  "Adjudication, not benchmarking: keep the lanes blind, compare decisions and quality dimensions across lanes on identical source IDs, and escalate every disagreement to a human reviewer with the evidence lists attached. No number in this record may be promoted to MEASURED without a named benchmark artifact.",
  ["A named benchmark artifact before any ESTIMATE in this batch is restated as MEASURED",
   "Cross-lane decision and quality-dimension comparison on identical source IDs",
   "A human adjudication record for every cross-lane disagreement",
   "Confirmation that this lane was produced without visibility into any other lane's corrected answers"],
  ["Treating provisional review output as expert gold would contaminate any downstream training or evaluation set",
   "Reviewer-generated ESTIMATE values can be mistaken for measurements once the labels are stripped",
   "This record says nothing about any model's domain capability; it is a corpus artifact, not a benchmark result"],
  0.66),
]
